+# ZS1-111 actual I/O, read cancellation and production CLI kill [_]
+
+Authority 3.1.19 / `3ce9613262de0a8499837571137782becc01daa7b56923a53b11f7f04058f60d`. Two-path candidate: src/tool_output.rs + tests/stage1_tool_output.rs. No core/headless/shared-main edit or acceptance checkmark.
+
+## Behavior and minimal repair
+
+A real host user-shell generated a complete default maximum 8MiB output artifact. Public Agent::read_tool_output requested only 8 bytes, then a separate thread set cancellation 1ms after the access precheck. Original read_range_inner authenticated/redacted the entire artifact without consulting cancellation. Measurement returned after 4847ms from cancellation. Adding a generous 1s regression ceiling produced an actual before failure: 0 passed / 1 failed, cancellation-to-return 4687ms (`read-cancel-before-fix.log`, SHA 10f6bc73edccd51f78b653e62b84a316a146299f8ec03bf0edaf3b6f49319d7c).
+
+The candidate passes the existing host cancellation callback into the internal reader and checks it before each bounded 8192B read/redaction chunk. Public raw/redacted host APIs retain their signatures. A cancelled scan returns OutputError::Cancelled; successful reads still authenticate the entire artifact, preserving the existing tamper and raw-coordinate rules. No core mutation was needed. Production delta is net +8 lines, 342 bytes. After: 1 passed / 0 failed, cancellation-to-return 4ms (`read-cancel-after-fix.log`, SHA d181a526c4f3c71e3cc7be9d4ae6b5b5430a525cabd50e8a1c0383ed3da7ca20). This is observed debug-build latency, not a cross-machine performance promise or an ability to interrupt an individual blocked kernel read.
+
+## Actual kernel I/O failures
+
+The EFBIG regression runs a separate test process, sets and restores its own RLIMIT_FSIZE/SIGXFSZ handler, and exercises a real partial write followed by kernel EFBIG without filling a disk. 4096 A bytes are followed by 16384 attempted B bytes: 20480 observed, 8192 stored, exact A4096+B4096 prefix/hash, finalized but incomplete/truncated. Independent process termination and store reopen preserve identity, verified prefix and failure status. This is a kernel I/O failure, not application quota simulation and not ENOSPC.
+
+The EACCES regression uses the current unprivileged UID and a private fixture directory: after real raw output is written, directory mode 0500 denies final metadata creation. The test restores 0700 with a guard. The actual errno is EACCES, complete remains false, the owner blocks further writes, reopening reports Interrupted and raw prefix is unchanged. This covers metadata publication permission failure, not permission changes to an already-open writable raw descriptor.
+
+Both passed; their actual-kernel-io run also executed four matching pre-existing actual behavior tests. No production I/O fix was needed beyond the separate cancellation repair.
+
+## Production CLI killed during an approved command
+
+`command_kill_probe.py` runs the built production CLI with a valid OpenAI configuration against a local HTTP observer. User-shell work makes zero HTTP requests. The CLI requests approval through JSONL, receives explicit allow, then starts the real bounded Python command. The command appends/fsyncs one START effect and emits stdout/stderr. The fixture waits for public tool_progress, sends SIGKILL to the owned CLI PID, records -9, then launches two further independent CLI owners.
+
+The durable journal contains tool_execution_started but no tool_execution_finished or sealed tool_output_captured. Two private stream artifacts retain raw prefixes with incomplete/unfinalized metadata. The new owner reports one generic unknown operation and one unknown tool entry correlated to the same operation/call. Original request ID stays unknown_outcome; conflicting reuse and fresh work before reconciliation are rejected. Original public events replay byte-for-byte. Finding orphan raw files does not confer public read authority: the public read is explicitly rejected as output_invalid. Explicit abandon is idempotent, produces no execution, then explicit session cleanup removes exactly two orphan streams. The third CLI owner has no pending recovery and still refuses to rerun the original request. The external START count stays exactly one.
+
+SIGKILL bypasses Rust cleanup. The fixture releases its own bounded child and records child-done before final inspection; this evidence does not claim the application automatically killed or reaped that child. `command-kill-production-config` passes, SHA 294f35971ce1d71fbc8245d21e4f79eb618693efbe04f2f290b8c564839884ce.
+
